let me ask you first Marv did you watch  mayweather vs mcgregor yes Chris I did  did not expect much particularly when  you get that type of wacky you know  promotional tour and my first impression  when they got into the ring I'm saying  to myself he McGregor looks bigger and  stronger than Mayweather and had a  tremendous reach advantage at a lefty  giving you different angles can be  tricky and then you think also Oh  Mayweather is 40 years old like talking  with Teddy Atlas at hearing Teddy's  opinions you know emphasizing that he's  40 and had fought in two years but I  think this is going to be a joke and it  turned out to be one of the more  compelling and action-packed fights in  some time the the four kings of the  1980s leonard Hagler duran and Hearns  guys that at one point did fight at 1:47  put mayweather in that mix where would  you rank him let's see you know I think  that sometimes your mind plays tricks on  you and you may go for the more exciting  fighter and Mayweather for the most part  despite his greatness and if you ask me  who would you rather see he would not be  on the top of that list as to who I  would rather watch I mean he was  brilliant and his defensive work but  what we saw the other night and not to  compare Mayweather against McGregor to  say Leonard and Hearns  and I was part of the pay-per-view team  that night doing that fight and I'll  never forget the fact that Leonard  actually changed up his styles got very  similar one Mayweather did asked around  for the other night because he was in  trouble and I'll never forget Angelo  Dundee in the corner just screaming at  Leonard and you know just getting him to  to get going and he  actually had Hearns a style and Hearns  was really fighting leonard style and  then Leonard you know came up with  victory you know by knocking down her  and that was pretty amazing but those  were two of the greatest fighters ovale  certainly of all time so for sure  excitement I would go with those guys  but Mayweather has to go down as you  know one of the extraordinary fighters  of all time for the most part strictly  from the defensive point view but hey  that's what you have to do talking to  Marv Albert NBN TNT joining me here on  the Dan Patrick show Marv the first time  you saw a dream team together what did  you think I got the chills when I first  saw them we did NBC at the time did the  tournament of Americans because they had  to qualify they had lost an 88 and so  they had to go through the tournament  was played in Portland and just the  first time they stepped onto the court  it was unbelievable to see that group  you know you look at is Michael Jordan  and Magic Johnson and Larry Bird and  Charles Barkley and Karl Malone and  right down the line you know David  Robinson Patrick Ewing to me it was a  greatest team in the history of the  greatest group in the history of team  sports ever assembled  I mean if everybody played today in that  type of competition and that means you  know having LeBron play and all the top  stars curry you know they they didn't  play in the Olympics as you know unreal  you could probably put together as close  to the dream team but it would not be  what this group was it'd be pretty close  which they never matched you know in the  Olympics after that but this this team  also was playing against international  teams that were not what they are today  I mean many of the international teams  as you saw the Olympics we you know to  give a run  to an NBA team because there's so many  NBA players it's so different and what  that particular Olympic tournament did  was encourage all the the basketball  strength in Europe and you know  throughout the world because people were  just fascinated with them they were like  the Beatles when they when they traveled  I mean just was fortunate enough to be  around them and they had top security  and couldn't even get on a bus without  thousands of people greeting them  outside the the hotel it became such a  big deal and I think the players  themselves were surprised about that and  really didn't go out much except from  for Charles Barkley who just you know he  just loves to talk to people and every  night he'd be walking down Las Ramblas  and Barcelona and just drawing crowds  and talking to everybody but that was a  team that Chuck Daly never called a  timeout throughout the whole Olympics  and they never trailed they had one  difficult once they did you know they  were gonna win but they they did have a  run in the gold medal game against  Croatia that I know envies well NBC's  carrying the entire tournament  and it opens with the Angola game in  which Berkeley threw that elbow which I  always thought was not as emphatic as  when you look at it because the rules in  the NBA led we're a little different in  terms of physical play and I you know  was unusual to see that in the Olympics  particularly right at the start but III  didn't even the Angolan players didn't  think it was that bad it's something  that I think has grown over the years  become legendary that Barkley threw an  elbow and the player from Angola but I  well people can see themselves tonight  before I let you go marbie legacy of  that team to me is that the world saw  that team embraced basketball and within  ten years really caught up you know the  USA team in o2 they got beat finished  six  in those World Championships you look at  the 92 team in its legacy what would you  say it is I would say that it changed  basketball because of the influence on  the International game everyone you  think how many international players are  in the NBA today it's somewhere over a  hundred and twenty five and they're  outstanding you know not all but I mean  you have stars within that group and you  know originally FIBA had been advocating  making the switch to professionals they  did it for years and originally the NBA  was not enthusiastic about it in fact  the US and Russia have voted against it  no one expected that you know they'd  become this cultural phenomenon and it's  something that they were convinced to do  and it turned out to be you know  something that became very very  successful but what I want to remember  most about it also it was really a lot  of goodwill because I remember a game  and the team was practicing in  Montecarlo at the start and I can recall  there was a guard for the French team  going at Michael Jordan he's probably  about five foot nine and he was pushing  him around and taunting him and Jordan  was really getting annoyed and as you  know you know he didn't need much for  motivation and here's this guy even  though the US was was really up by a big  score against France this guy was all  over him and they're screaming at each  other then the game ends and the United  States went by a large margin the guy  comes over with his own photographer  puts his arm around Jordan and asked for  a picture and they took a picture photo  right after that and that was that kind  of set the tone after games at the  Olympics the players would take photos  with the US team which was a lot always  I got a kick out of saying that well  dream team that was good there was good  will spread all so let's let's say  despite the fact that most of the games  were were blowouts well dream team week  begins tonight the Olympic Channel home  of Team USA all  original broadcast on eight consecutive  nights in primetime Marv Albert on the  call for those games beginning tonight  8:30 p.m. Eastern Time ARP as always  thanks for joining here on the show hey  Chris the pleasure of anytime  that's Marv Albert NBN TNT part of the  92 Olympics broadcast team of course  back again in 2016 in Rio for the  broadcast of those Olympics the Dan  Patrick show weekday mornings on  audience

